window.PHASE_DATA_LN=window.PHASE_DATA_LN||{};window.PHASE_DATA_LN[3]={"pages":[{"pdf":64,"printed":49,"ch":3,"text":"CHAPTER 3\n\nLayer 2: Climate Economics and Discounting\nBefore you start: what you need to know first\nLayer 2 is one argument told five ways: how much should we spend today to avoid climate damage in the distant, uncertain future? Everything turns on three ideas, so learn these before the papers. The papers themselves do not teach them.\n\n1. Discounting and the Ramsey rule\nA benefit in the future is worth less today, and the ”discount rate” says how much less. The standard split of that rate is the Ramsey rule: r = ρ + ηg\n• r is the consumption discount rate (the number the whole debate is about).\n• ρ is the pure rate of time preference: how much you value a future person less just for being in the future.\n• η is the elasticity of marginal utility of consumption: how fast an extra rupee matters less as people get richer. It is also a measure of inequality aversion.\n• g is the growth rate of consumption per person.\nThe richer future people are expected to be, the less a rupee given to them is worth, so a higher g or η raises r. Stern argues for a tiny ρ (about 0.1% a year) and so a low r. Nordhaus argues for a higher ρ and η chosen to match market rates, and so a higher r. Most of their disagreement on how much to cut emissions comes from this one choice, not from climate science.\n\n2. Utility curvature and risk aversion\nIn these models utility is usually a power (CRRA) function of consumption, and the same η does two jobs: it sets how much you dislike inequality across time and how much you dislike risk. This is a known weakness, and Weitzman and Pindyck both lean on it.\n\n3. Fat tails\nA ”thin-tailed” distribution makes extreme outcomes so unlikely that they barely affect averages. A ”fat-tailed” one leaves enough probability on catastrophe that the extreme outcomes dominate the expected loss. Weitzman's argument is that climate sensitivity is fat-tailed because we are uncertain about the uncertainty itself."},{"pdf":65,"printed":50,"ch":3,"text":"Map of Layer 2\nTable 3.1. Before you start: Map of Layer 2\nSection\n\nSource\n\nOne-line job\n\nStern (2006), the Stern\nReview\n\nThe case for early, strong action, with a low discount rate.\n\nNordhaus (2017),\nDICE-2016R\n\nThe standard integrated-assessment model; a central social\ncost of carbon of about US$31 per tonne.\n\nWeitzman (2009), with\nthe 2007 draft\n\nThe Dismal Theorem: fat tails can matter more than the\ndiscount rate.\n\nPindyck (2013)\n\nIntegrated assessment models give false precision; use\nplausible scenarios instead.\n\nMaster list\n\nThe quotes to reach for first and how this layer feeds the\nthesis.\n\nHow Layer 2 connects to the rest: it answers how much mitigation and when, which is the policy shock that Layer 1's scenarios assume and that Layers 3 to 6 turn into bank losses. It does not mention banks.\n\n1. Stern (2006), The Economics of Climate Change: the Stern Review\nSource: Stern, N. (2006), Stern Review: The Economics of Climate Change, HM Treasury, 662 PDF pages (your file ”Stern (2006).pdf”). Page references use the Review's own numbering: roman numerals for the Executive Summary (”ES p. x”), arabic for the chapters. What I read. I read the Executive Summary in full, Chapter 2 (economics, ethics and climate change) and its technical annex on discounting, and the integrated-assessment section of Chapter 6 (pp. 160–161). I searched the rest for the figures quoted below. I did not read the 600 pages of Parts II to VI (impacts by sector, stabilisation costs by technology, policy design, international action). The Review is a UK Treasury report, not a journal paper. Its job was to give a policymaker a cost-benefit case for acting early. Almost every later paper in this layer is a reply to it.\n\nThe argument in six steps\n1. Climate change is a market failure on an unprecedented scale. ES p. i: it is ”the greatest and widest-ranging market failure ever seen”, and the economics must be global, long-horizon, built around risk, and open to ”major, non-marginal change”.\n2. Risks rise sharply with the stock of gases. The stock was about 430 ppm CO2e against 280 ppm before the Industrial Revolution (ES p. iii). At 550 ppm CO2e there is ”at least a 77% chance” of exceeding 2°C (ES p. iii). Under business as usual (BAU) the stock could ”more than treble” by 2100, giving ”at least a 50% risk” of exceeding 5°C (ES p. iv)."},{"pdf":66,"printed":51,"ch":3,"text":"3. The cost of inaction is large. Taking account of risk, the damage from BAU climate change is ”equivalent to an average reduction in global per-capita consumption of at least 5%, now and forever” (ES p. x). Adding non-market impacts (health, environment) raises it to 11%; a more responsive climate to 14%; equity weights for poor regions to about 20% (ES p. x). The Review's range is 5 to 20% and it says the right figure is ”likely to be in the upper part of this range” (ES p. x).\n4. The cost of action is small. Stabilising at 500 to 550 ppm CO2e would cost ”around 1% of GDP by 2050”, with a range of -1% to +3.5% from the resource-cost method and -2% to +5% from macro models (ES p. xii–xiv).\n5. So act now. ”The benefits of strong, early action considerably outweigh the costs” (ES p. ii). The Review adds that there is ”a high price to delay” (ES p. xv).\n6. How much action? Aim for stabilisation in the range of 450 to 550 ppm CO2e (ES p. xvii). ”Uncertainty is an argument for a more, not less, demanding goal” (ES p. xvii).\n\nWhy the discount rate matters so much here\nThe Review argues that the pure rate of time preference should be near zero. It makes this an ethical point in Chapter 2:\n• Its simple approach is: ”if a future generation will be present, we suppose that it has the same claim on our ethical attention as the current one” (p. 30).\n• It keeps a small δ only for the chance that a catastrophe (the ”meteorite” example) ends the world. The value used is 0.1% a year, so the chance of surviving 100 years is 90.5% (Ch. 6, p. 160).\n• It sets η = 1, so utility is the log of consumption (Ch. 6, p. 160).\n• Ramsey, Pigou and Harrod all called pure time discounting indefensible, in the Review's quotations (p. 30–31). A low δ does not mean a low discount rate: ”using a low δ does not imply a low discount rate”, and with η = 1.5 and growth of 2.5% the discount rate would be 3.75% even if δ = 0 (p. 47). The Review also notes that the standard single discount rate is only valid for small changes around a given path, not for comparing very different futures (Box 2.2, p. 31).\n\nOther numbers worth having\nTable 3.2. Stern (2006): Other numbers worth having\nItem\n\nFigure\n\nPage\n\nSocial cost of carbon on BAU path\n\nabout US$85 per tonne of CO2\n\nES p. xvi\n\nSocial cost of carbon on a 450–550 ppm path US$25–30 per tonne, about a third of BAU ES p. xvii Net benefit of shifting to the better path\n\norder of US$2.5 trillion (present value)\n\nES p. xvii\n\nAnnual cost of extreme weather by\nmid-century\n\n0.5–1% of world GDP, from simple\nextrapolation\n\nES p. viii\n\nClimate-change cost with 2–3°C warming in\nolder models\n\npermanent loss of about 0–3% of world\noutput\n\nES p. ix"},{"pdf":67,"printed":52,"ch":3,"text":"Item\n\nFigure\n\nPage\n\nClimate-change cost with 5–6°C, models\nwith abrupt change\n\naverage 5–10% loss of world GDP, poor\ncountries over 10%\n\nES p. ix\n\nPeak and decline for 550 ppm CO2e\n\nemissions peak within 10–20 years, fall\n1–3% a year\n\nES p. xi\n\nPower sector decarbonisation by 2050\n\nat least 60%, maybe 75%\n\nES p. xiii\n\nWhat the Review says that touches finance and India\n• Finance (one sentence only): at higher temperatures ”the rising costs of extreme weather events could affect global financial markets through higher and more volatile costs of insurance” (ES p. viii). The Review does not analyse banks.\n• India: glacier melt ”eventually threatening one-sixth of the world's population, predominantly in the Indian sub-continent” (ES p. vi). Warming may cause sudden shifts in regional weather patterns ”such as the monsoon rains in South Asia” (ES p. vii).\n• Fossil fuels: reserves profitable to extract ”are more than enough to take the world to levels of greenhouse-gas concentrations well beyond 750ppm CO2e” (ES p. xiv). This is the other face of the stranded-asset argument you saw in Layer 1.\n\nQuotables\nTable 3.3. Stern (2006): Quotables\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\nClimate change is ”the greatest and\nwidest-ranging market failure ever seen”\n\nES p. i\n\nOpening the case for a policy-driven\ntransition\n\n”The benefits of strong, early action\nconsiderably outweigh the costs.”\n\nES p. ii Citing the founding cost-benefit case for\nearly action\n\n”Averaging across possibilities conceals risks.”\n\nES p.\n\nJustifying a stress-test or tail-risk\napproach instead of expected losses only\n\n”Uncertainty is an argument for a more, not\nless, demanding goal”\n\nES p.\nxvii\n\nArguing that uncertainty strengthens, not\nweakens, the case for early action\n\n”there is a high price to delay”\n\nES p.\n\nSupporting a disorderly or late-transition\nscenario\n\n”if you care little about future generations you\nwill care little about climate change”\n\np. 47\n\nExplaining why the discount rate is an\nethical choice\n\n”the rising costs of extreme weather events\ncould affect global financial markets through\nhigher and more volatile costs of insurance”\n\nES p.\nviii\n\nAn early official statement of the financial\nchannel (one sentence only)\n\n”the results are specific to the model and its\nassumptions. They should not be endowed with\na precision and certainty that is simply\nimpossible to achieve.”\n\nES p. x Quoting the authors' own caution about\nthe numbers"},{"pdf":68,"printed":53,"ch":3,"text":"What it means for your thesis\n• The founding policy argument. If your thesis says that a policy-driven transition is coming and that banks face transition risk, the Stern Review is the source that made ”act early” the mainstream economic position. It is the reason you can treat a tightening of climate policy as plausible rather than speculative (my reading).\n• Contested numbers. The 5–20% and 1% of GDP figures are the most cited and the most criticised in this layer. Use them with the date (2006) and with the criticism from Nordhaus, Weitzman and Pindyck in the following sections.\n• The ”price to delay” idea. Late and sharp action costs more than early and gradual action. This is the economic logic behind a disorderly scenario in central bank stress tests (the orderly/disorderly labels are a central-bank convention, not Stern's wording).\n• A thin finance link. One sentence on insurance and financial markets. Do not cite Stern for anything about banks. Can you skip the original? For the argument, the discount-rate stance and the headline numbers, yes. Open these places yourself: the Executive Summary (ES p. i–xxvii, about 27 pages and readable), Box 2.2 (p. 31) on when discounting is valid, and Chapter 6 pp. 160–161 for the model settings. I did not read Parts II to VI. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.\n\n2. Nordhaus (2017), Revisiting the social cost of carbon (the DICE\nmodel)\nSource: Nordhaus, W. D. (2017), ”Revisiting the social cost of carbon”, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 114(7): 1518–1523 (your file ”Nordhaus Dice 2013.pdf”). The file name is misleading: this is the 2017 paper on the revised DICE-2016R model, not a 2013 paper. The 2013 version (DICE-2013R) is only cited here. If you meant the 2013 model, tell me and we can look for that paper. Page references are the PNAS page numbers. What I read. All six pages, including every table. It is short enough that you could read the original in an hour. Nordhaus is the standard counterweight to Stern. Both use a model of an economy growing over time with climate damage subtracted. They differ mostly in how they treat the discount rate.\n\nVocabulary\nTable 3.4. Nordhaus (2017): Vocabulary\nTerm\n\nMeaning in this paper\n\nSocial cost of carbon (SCC)\n\n”the change in the discounted value of economic welfare from an\nadditional unit of CO2-equivalent emissions” (p. 1518). In plain\nterms, the damage done by one more tonne of CO2.\n\nIAM\n\nIntegrated assessment model: economy, carbon cycle, climate and\ndamages in one system (p. 1518)."},{"pdf":69,"printed":54,"ch":3,"text":"Term\n\nMeaning in this paper\n\nDICE\n\nDynamic Integrated model of Climate and the Economy; a Ramsey\ngrowth model with climate investment added (p. 1518).\n\nWelfare discount rate (ρ) vs\ngoods discount rate (r)\n\nρ applies to the well-being of different generations and is not\nobserved; r is the return on capital and is observed in markets (p.\n1520).\n\nGrowth-corrected discount rate\n\nThe goods discount rate minus the growth rate of consumption; the\nkey variable that drives the SCC (p. 1522).\n\nDescriptive vs prescriptive\ndiscounting\n\nDescriptive: choose parameters so that the model reproduces\nobserved savings and returns on capital (Nordhaus). Prescriptive:\nchoose the discount rate on ethical grounds (Stern) (p. 1520).\n\nHow the model works, briefly\n• It maximises a social welfare function: the discounted sum of population-weighted utility of consumption, with a power utility function (p. 1518).\n• Output is reduced by climate damage and abatement costs, so society trades off consumption now against less damage later (p. 1518–1519).\n• Damages are a quadratic function of global mean temperature. The paper says the model ”takes globally averaged temperature change (TAT) as a sufficient statistic for damages” (p. 1519).\n• Damages come out at 2.1% of global income at 3°C and 8.5% at 6°C (p. 1519).\n• Equilibrium climate sensitivity is set at 3.1°C for a doubling of CO2 (p. 1520).\n• The discount rate averages 4¼% a year to 2100, chosen so the model matches observed returns on investment (p. 1520).\n\nThe main results\nTable 3.5. Nordhaus (2017): The main results\nResult\n\nFigure\n\nPage\n\nSCC for 2015 emissions, central\ncase\n\nUS$31.2 per tonne CO2 (2010 US$)\n\np. 1518, Table\n\nGrowth of the SCC\n\nabout 3% a year in real terms to 2050; US$102.5\nin 2050\n\np. 1520, Table\n\nSCC if warming is capped at\n2.5°C\n\nUS$184 (hard cap); US$107 (cap on 100-year\naverage)\n\np. 1522, Table\n\nSCC with Stern-style\ndiscounting\n\nUS$197 for 2015\n\np. 1520, Table\n\nSCC at a 2.5% discount rate on\ngoods\n\nUS$128.5\n\np. 1520, Table\n\nSCC at a 5% discount rate on\ngoods\n\nUS$19.7\n\np. 1520, Table\n\nStandard deviation of the 2015\nSCC\n\nUS$32; 10th to 90th percentile range US$7 to\nUS$77\n\np. 1522"},{"pdf":70,"printed":55,"ch":3,"text":"Result\n\nFigure\n\nPage\n\nChange from DICE-2013R\n\nUS$17 to US$31, mainly from the carbon cycle\nand economic assumptions\n\np. 1522, Table\n\nThe table of discount rates shows the point of this layer: the same model with a different discount rate gives an SCC anywhere from about US$20 to US$200. The disagreement between Stern and Nordhaus is mostly about this one input.\n\nIndia\nTable 2 (p. 1521) splits the global SCC by region using output shares. India gets US$2.93 of the US$31.21 (a 9% share in this study; the older RICE, FUND and PAGE models give 12%, 5% and 22%). Nordhaus draws the conclusion that ”there is little agreement on the distribution of the SCC by region” while each country's SCC is ”well below the global total” (p. 1522). The paper also says that damages in poor regions ”have proven most difficult to develop firm estimates for” (p. 1523).\n\nQuotables\nTable 3.6. Nordhaus (2017): Quotables\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\n”The most important single economic concept in 1518\nthe economics of climate change is the social cost\nof carbon (SCC).”\n\nIntroducing the SCC as the link between\nclimate damage and carbon pricing\n\nThe SCC is ”the change in the discounted value\nof economic welfare from an additional unit of\nCO2-equivalent emissions”\n\nDefining the SCC\n\n1518\n\n”The DICE-2016R model takes globally averaged 1519\ntemperature change (TAT) as a sufficient\nstatistic for damages.”\n\nStating a limitation of aggregate IAMs\n(no sector or bank-level detail)\n\nThe goods discount rate ”should reflect actual\neconomic outcomes”\n\n1520\n\nContrasting the descriptive and\nprescriptive approaches to discounting\n\n”These estimates confirm that there is extremely\nlarge structural uncertainty about the SCC even\nin a single model.”\n\n1522\n\nSupporting a scenario-based rather than\na point-estimate approach\n\n”the regional estimates are poorly understood,\noften varying by a factor of 2 across the three\nmodels”\n\n1522\n\nCaution on using regional or\nIndia-specific SCC numbers\n\nRegulations with benefits of more than US$1\ntrillion ”use the SCC” (Significance box)\n\n1518\n\nThe SCC is used in real policy, so its\nlevel matters\n\nWhat it means for your thesis\n• A price path for carbon. The SCC is the carbon price that an optimising government would eventually set. Transition-risk scenarios need a carbon price path, and this paper gives you a defensible central value (about US$31 in 2015, rising about 3% a year) and"},{"pdf":71,"printed":56,"ch":3,"text":"a range (US$20 to US$200 depending on discounting). Check how the scenario sets you will use in Layers 3 to 6 choose their carbon prices before relying on this (my suggestion; the paper itself does not discuss scenarios for banks).\n• Why scenarios disagree. Differences between a gentle and an aggressive policy scenario can come from the discount rate alone, not from the physics.\n• The aggregation limitation. A single global temperature drives all damages. There is no sector, no region of India and no financial sector. That gap is where your thesis sits.\n• Not about banks. There is nothing on finance or capital adequacy.\nCan you skip the original? For the argument, yes. It is only six pages, so I would read Table 1 (p. 1520), Fig. 3 (p. 1520) and Table 4 (p. 1522) yourself because you may quote them. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.\n\n3. Weitzman (2009), On modeling and interpreting the economics\nof catastrophic climate change (the Dismal Theorem)\nSource: Weitzman, M. L. (2009), ”On Modeling and Interpreting the Economics of Catastrophic Climate Change”, Review of Economics and Statistics 91(1): 1–19 (your file ”weitzman2009.pdf”). Your second Weitzman file, ”Weitzman_2007.pdf”, is the November 2007 working-paper draft of this same paper (44 pages), not his separate review of the Stern Review. I treat the two together here and cite the published page numbers. The differences are listed below. What I read. The published paper in full (19 pages). I skimmed the 2007 draft for differences and did not read it line by line. The proof of the theorem is only a sketch in the paper, and I have not checked the mathematics. This is the paper that makes the strongest case that the discount rate fight between Stern and Nordhaus is not the main issue. For a thesis on bank capital, its value is a way of thinking about tail risk and about why expected-loss numbers can mislead.\n\nVocabulary\nTable 3.7. Weitzman (2009): Vocabulary\nTerm\n\nMeaning\n\nCost-benefit analysis\n(CBA)\n\nComparing discounted benefits and costs of a policy using expected utility.\n\nFat tail\n\nA distribution whose extreme outcomes die away more slowly than\nexponentially, e.g. a power law (p. 2, fn 2). Thin tails (normal, gamma) die\naway faster.\n\nStructural uncertainty\n\nUncertainty about the parameters and form of the model itself, not just the\nrandom outcome within a known model.\n\nClimate sensitivity (S1)\n\nEventual warming from a doubling of CO2. IPCC AR4: likely 2°C to 4.5°C,\nbest estimate 3°C, values above 4.5°C ”cannot be excluded” (p. 3).\n\nWeitzman's ”generalized” sensitivity that adds heat-triggered feedbacks such\nas methane release (p. 4)."},{"pdf":72,"printed":57,"ch":3,"text":"Term\n\nMeaning\n\nStochastic discount\nfactor (M)\n\nThe weight on future consumption in expected-utility pricing: M = β\nexp(−ηY). E[M] is the price today of a sure unit of future consumption (p.\n6–7).\n\nVSL-like parameter (λ)\n\nA ”value of statistical life” generalised to catastrophe: how many times\nper-capita consumption society would give up to avoid extinction-scale loss.\nIt sets a floor on consumption (p. 9–10).\n\nDismal Theorem\n\nIf λ can be very large, E[M] goes to +∞ (Theorem 1, p. 10).\n\nThe argument\n1. The problem is not discounting alone. The uniqueness of climate change is the ”deep structural uncertainty in the science coupled with an economic inability to evaluate meaningfully the catastrophic losses from disastrous temperature changes” (p. 1).\n2. Tail probabilities are crude but not small enough to ignore. Averaging 22 studies behind IPCC AR4, the upper 5% level of climate sensitivity is about 7°C (p. 3). Adding heat-triggered feedbacks (Torn and Harte, whose numbers Weitzman uses), the 5% level rises to about 11.5°C and the 1% level to about 22.6°C (p. 4). He rounds this to a base case: P[ΔT > 10°C] ≈ 5% and P[ΔT > 20°C] ≈ 1% (p. 4).\n3. Learning cannot thin the tail. Because a scale parameter is unknown and we have finite data, the ”posterior-predictive” distribution has a power-law tail whatever the original distribution was (p. 11). Fat tails come from structural uncertainty alone and do not need to be assumed.\n4. The Dismal Theorem. The probability of a disaster of scale s falls only polynomially in s, but the marginal-utility cost rises exponentially. ”The race to the bottom of the bad tail” is ”won in the limit every time by the marginal-utility impact” (p. 11). So expected marginal utility is unbounded unless we close the model with a bound on consumption.\n5. Closing the model makes the answer depend on λ. With η ≈ 2 and λ ≈ 100 the\nconsumption floor is about 1% of current consumption; with λ ≈ 1,000 about 0.1% (p. 10). For λ ≈ 1,000 and a catastrophe probability of 0.005, willingness to pay to avoid it is about 83% of consumption (p. 15).\n6. So discounting is secondary. Theorem 1 ”seems to be saying that no matter what values of β or η are selected” any big-λ CBA should be presumed affected by fat tails (p. 15). ”The debate about discounting may be secondary to a debate about the open-ended catastrophic reach of climate disasters” (p. 15).\n7. A modest policy conclusion. Weitzman does not give a number. The theorem points to ”a relatively more cautious approach to GHG emissions” (p. 13), more effort to learn how fat the tail is, and the idea of climate policy as catastrophe insurance (p. 18). He also warns economists against ”misplaced concreteness” (p. 18).\n\nThe generalised Ramsey formula\nThe paper uses the well-known risk-adjusted version of the Ramsey rule from the ”Before you start” section above:"},{"pdf":73,"printed":58,"ch":3,"text":"rf = δ + ηµ − 12 η 2 s2\nHere δ is pure time preference, ημ is the growth term, and the last term is a precautionarysaving effect: more uncertainty about consumption (a larger s) lowers the risk-free rate (p. 7). Weitzman says the effect of δ ”is theoretically overshadowed by the effect of the uncertain scaling parameter s” (p. 7).\n\nHow the 2007 draft differs from the published paper\n• The draft uses only one base-case tail: P[ΔT > 20°C] ≈ 1%. The published paper adds P[ΔT > 10°C] ≈ 5% (draft introduction and section 2; published p. 1 and p. 4).\n• The section headings and the Dismal Theorem section match; I did not compare the wording line by line.\n• The core theorem and model are the same. Cite the published version in the thesis.\n\nQuotables\nTable 3.8. Weitzman (2009): Quotables\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\nFat-tailed structural uncertainty ”can readily\noutweigh the effects of discounting in\nclimate-change policy analysis”\n\n1 (abstract) Arguing that expected-loss analysis\nwith thin tails understates tail risk\n\n”fat-tailed CBA can turn conventional\nthin-tail-based climate-change policy advice on\nits head”\n\nJustifying a tail-risk or stress-test\napproach over central estimates\n\n”structural or deep uncertainty is potentially\nmuch more of a driving force than discounting\nor pure risk”\n\nSame, and for distancing your thesis\nfrom the discount-rate debate\n\nThe tail probabilities are ”wildly uncertain,\nunbelievably crude ballpark estimates—most\ndefinitely not based on hard science”\n\nBeing honest about the status of\nextreme-tail numbers\n\n”the more speculative and fuzzy are the tiny tail 5\nprobabilities of extreme events, the less\nignorable and the more serious is the impact on\npresent discounted expected utility for a\nrisk-averse agent”\n\nThe central counter-intuitive result\n\n”expected stochastic discount factors—which lie\nat the heart of cost-benefit, asset-pricing, and\nmany other important applications of EU\ntheory—can become arbitrarily large”\n\nA link to asset-pricing (Layer 5)\n\n”the artificial crispness conveyed by\nconventional IAM-based CBAs here is especially\nand unusually misleading”\n\nCriticising point estimates from\nintegrated assessment models\n\n”we may be deluding ourselves and others with\nmisplaced concreteness”\n\nSame"},{"pdf":74,"printed":59,"ch":3,"text":"What it means for your thesis\n• A basis for tail-risk framing. If you argue that a bank's capital should be tested against severe but plausible climate scenarios and not only against expected losses, Weitzman gives a formal reason: with deep uncertainty, an average can be dominated by the tail. This is my reading of how the result can be used; Weitzman is writing about global welfare, not bank losses.\n• A caution against over-claiming. The paper is about civilisation-scale catastrophe. A bank's loan book is a limited-exposure portfolio, and Weitzman himself says that for ”idiosyncratic uncertainty” that ”affects only one small part” of a portfolio, exposure is naturally limited and ”bad-tail fatness is not such a paramount concern” (p. 11). Do not stretch the Dismal Theorem to say that bank losses are unbounded.\n• A bridge to Layers 5 and 6. The stochastic discount factor is the core object of asset pricing (Cochrane, Layer 5), and the ”insurance” framing is the logic behind the central-bank climate stress tests in Layer 6.\n• India. The only mention is a passing remark that India and China might industrialise faster than projected (p. 4). Can you skip the original? For the idea and its implications, yes. The mathematics (Sections III to V, p. 6–12) is hard going and I would not expect you to follow all of it, but you should read the Introduction (p. 1–2), Section VI (p. 12–15) and the Conclusion (p. 18) yourself because they are written in plain language and contain most of the quotes. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.\n\n4. Pindyck (2013), Climate change policy: what do the models tell\nus?\nSource: Pindyck, R. S. (2013), ”Climate Change Policy: What Do the Models Tell Us?”, NBER Working Paper 19244, July 2013 (your file ”Pindyck (2013).pdf”; the published version is in the Journal of Economic Literature, September 2013). Page references are the workingpaper page numbers, which are 1 to 17 starting at the Introduction. What I read. All of it, including the footnotes. It is 17 pages of text and is written in plain language, so it is the easiest paper in this layer to read directly. Pindyck's answer to the question in the title is the first word of the abstract: ”Very little.” He is not arguing against climate action. He is arguing that the integrated assessment models (IAMs) that produce numbers like Nordhaus's SCC give false precision.\n\nThe argument\n1. The inputs are arbitrary and decide the output. Stern and Nordhaus got very different SCCs because ”the inputs that go into the models are so different” (p. 4–5). Pindyck reports that Nordhaus (2008) finds an SCC around US$20 or less while Stern finds one above US$200 (p. 4). The modeller ”has a great deal of freedom”, so ”these models can be used to obtain almost any result one desires” (p. 5).\n2. Discount parameters cannot be pinned down. The standard Ramsey discount rate is R = δ + ηg (p. 7). Stern sets δ ≈ 0 and η = 1, so R is small. Nordhaus (2008)"},{"pdf":75,"printed":60,"ch":3,"text":"matches market data with δ = 1.5% and η = 2, so R ≈ 5.5% (p. 7). Pindyck calls the rate of time preference ”a policy parameter” and says economists ”have little to say” about whether discounting future generations is unethical (p. 6). He notes that the US Interagency Working Group sidestepped the issue with a middle rate of 3% (p. 7–8).\n3. Climate sensitivity is uncertain and probably more so than modellers allow. The IPCC's 22 studies give about 7°C at the 95th percentile (p. 9). The strength of the feedbacks behind this is largely unknown and, Pindyck says, may even be unknowable for the foreseeable future (p. 8–9). Modellers who calibrate a single distribution ”have understated our uncertainty” (p. 10).\n4. Damage functions are made up. IAMs relate temperature to GDP with a loss function such as DICE's inverse-quadratic. There is no theory for its shape; the parameters are picked so that losses are small at 2–4°C, where there is ”a rough consensus (perhaps completely wrong)” (p. 13). Putting 5°C or 7°C into the function ”is a completely meaningless exercise” (p. 13).\n5. The most important driver is the one the models cannot see. The possibility of a catastrophic outcome is ”an essential driver of the SCC”, and ”IAMs cannot tell us anything about catastrophic outcomes” (p. 14).\n6. What to do instead. Pindyck proposes a simpler approach, like Cold War nuclear risk analysis: set out a plausible range of catastrophic outcomes (e.g., percentage falls in the capital stock) and plausible probabilities, then compute the present value of averting them (p. 15). Policy is ”a form of insurance” (p. 16).\n7. A practical conclusion. Not to wait. He points to the argument, citing his own work, that the Interagency Working Group's US$21 (updated to US$33) number is a ”rough and politically acceptable starting point” for a carbon tax, to be revised as we learn more (p. 16–17).\n\nWhere he disagrees with Weitzman\nPindyck agrees with Weitzman that catastrophe matters most, but not that fat tails alone settle the issue. A footnote says Weitzman (2009) ”has shown that parameter uncertainty can lead to a fat-tailed distribution” and that this ”suggests that the value of abatement is high”, but Pindyck (2011a) ”shows that a fat-tailed distribution by itself need not imply a high value of abatement” (p. 10, fn 16).\n\nA point on growth versus level effects\nPindyck argues that temperature should affect the growth rate of GDP, not only its level, because some damage is permanent and adaptation spending crowds out investment (p. 12). He cites Dell, Jones and Olken (2012), whose panel of 136 countries over 50 years finds that higher temperatures reduce GDP growth rates but not levels, and Bansal and Ochoa (2011, 2012), who find a negative effect of temperature increases on economic growth (p. 12). I have not read those papers; I only report what Pindyck says they show.\n\nFlag: SCC numbers\nPindyck quotes an SCC ”above US$200” for Stern (p. 4). The Stern Review's own headline figure was about US$85 per tonne (Section 1 above, ES p. xvi). The US$200 level matches"},{"pdf":76,"printed":61,"ch":3,"text":"Nordhaus's own run with Stern's discounting (US$197 in Nordhaus 2017, Table 1). When you cite SCC numbers, say whose model and which assumptions produced them.\n\nQuotables\nTable 3.9. Pindyck (2013): Quotables\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nPage\n\nWhere it could be used\n\n”IAM-based analyses of climate policy create a\nperception of knowledge and precision, but that\nperception is illusory and misleading.”\n\nabstract\n\nCriticising point estimates from\nintegrated assessment models\n\n”these models can be used to obtain almost any\nresult one desires”\n\nExplaining why Stern and Nordhaus\ndisagree\n\nThe rate of time preference is ”a policy\nparameter”\n\nTreating the discount rate as a choice\nrather than a fact\n\n”the damage functions used in most IAMs are\ncompletely made up, with no theoretical or\nempirical foundation”\n\nJustifying why you do not rely on\naggregate damage functions\n\n”IAMs cannot tell us anything about catastrophic\noutcomes, and thus cannot provide meaningful\nestimates of the SCC”\n\nSupporting a scenario or stress-test\napproach\n\n”it may be that the best we can do is rely on the\n‘plausible.’”\n\nDefending plausible-scenario analysis\nagainst false precision\n\n”One can think of a GHG abatement policy as a\nform of insurance”\n\nLinking climate policy to insurance\nlogic (see also Weitzman)\n\nHigher temperatures ”reduce GDP growth rates\nbut not levels” (Pindyck's summary of Dell, Jones\nand Olken 2012)\n\nLinking warming to borrowers' income\ngrowth\n\nWhat it means for your thesis\n• A defence for using scenarios. If an examiner asks why you do not use a modeloptimal carbon price or damage function, Pindyck gives a published reason: the aggregate models rest on arbitrary choices, so a plausible-range approach is more honest. The scenario sets used by central banks follow the same logic (my reading; Pindyck does not discuss banks).\n• A caution on every number in this layer. The SCC values of US$10 to US$200,\nStern's 5–20% and Nordhaus's 2.1% at 3°C are all model outputs. Cite them as such.\n• A pointer to the growth channel. If temperature lowers growth, borrower incomes and repayment capacity suffer over time. That is a physical-risk channel to banks, but you would need the original empirical papers to rely on it.\n• Nothing on finance directly. The paper does not mention banks or capital.\nCan you skip the original? For the argument, yes. Since it is short and clear, I would still read sections 2.2 (p. 5–8) and 3.2 (p. 11–13) yourself. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis."},{"pdf":77,"printed":62,"ch":3,"text":"5. Master quotables list and how Layer 2 feeds the thesis\nLayer 2 is a four-way argument about how to price climate risk when the future is uncertain. Here is how the four papers line up, followed by the ten quotes I would reach for first.\n\nThe four positions side by side\nTable 3.10. Master quotables list and how Layer 2 feeds the thesis: The four positions side by side Stern (2006) Main claim\n\nNordhaus (2017)\n\nBenefits of strong, early Central SCC of about\naction outweigh costs\nUS$31 per tonne for\n2015, rising about 3% a\nyear\n\nWeitzman (2009)\n\nPindyck (2013)\n\nFat-tailed\nuncertainty can\noutweigh\ndiscounting\n\nIAMs give false\nprecision\n\nArbitrary; a policy\nparameter (p. 6)\n\nDiscounting\nδ = 0.1%, η = 1 (Ch.\n6, p. 160)\n\nChosen to match\nmarket returns; about\n4¼% a year (p. 1520)\n\nδ is overshadowed\nby the effect of\nuncertainty (p. 7,\n15)\n\nTreatmentModels risk explicitly;\nof tails ”averaging across\npossibilities conceals\nrisks” (ES p. ix)\n\nReports uncertainty:\nSD of US$32,\n10th–90th percentile\nUS$7–77 (p. 1522)\n\nCentral: the Dismal Central, but IAMs\nTheorem (p. 10)\n”cannot tell us\nanything” about it\n(p. 14)\n\nCarbon price at the\nPolicy Aim for 450–550 ppm\nimplica- CO2e; cost about 1% of SCC\nGDP (ES p. xii, xvii)\ntion\n\nMore caution;\npolicy as\ncatastrophe\ninsurance (p. 13,\n18)\n\nTreat policy as\ninsurance; use\nplausible scenarios\n(p. 15–16)\n\nWhere they agree. Carbon should be priced; uncertainty is large; and nobody here analyses banks or capital.\n\nThe ten quotes to reach for first\nTable 3.11. Master quotables list and how Layer 2 feeds the thesis: The ten quotes to reach for first Job in the thesis\n\nSource and\npage\n\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nOpening the case for Climate change is ”the greatest and widest-ranging market policy failure ever seen”\n\nStern, ES p. i\n\nDiscounting is ethics ”if you care little about future generations you will care little about climate change”\n\nStern, p. 47\n\nAverages hide tails\n\n”Averaging across possibilities conceals risks.”\n\nStern, ES p.\n\nDefining the SCC\n\n”the change in the discounted value of economic welfare from\nan additional unit of CO2-equivalent emissions”\n\nNordhaus, p.\n1518\n\nModel uncertainty\n\n”extremely large structural uncertainty about the SCC even in\na single model”\n\nNordhaus, p.\n1522"},{"pdf":78,"printed":63,"ch":3,"text":"Job in the thesis\n\nQuote (short, verbatim)\n\nSource and\npage\n\nTails over\ndiscounting\n\n”structural or deep uncertainty is potentially much more of a\ndriving force than discounting or pure risk”\n\nWeitzman, p.\n\nFuzzy tails still\nmatter\n\n”the more speculative and fuzzy are the tiny tail probabilities of Weitzman, p. extreme events, the less ignorable and the more serious is the impact”\n\nMisplaced\nconcreteness\n\n”we may be deluding ourselves and others with misplaced\nconcreteness”\n\nWeitzman, p.\n\nModels can say\nanything\n\n”these models can be used to obtain almost any result one\ndesires”\n\nPindyck, p. 5\n\nDamage functions\n\n”completely made up, with no theoretical or empirical\nfoundation”\n\nPindyck, p.\n\nFor exact wording and context, see the quotables table in each section above.\n\nHow Layer 2 feeds the thesis\n1. It explains why the scenario numbers disagree. Differences in carbon prices and damage estimates come mostly from the discount rate and the damage function, not from physics. When you present scenarios from Layer 1 or from central banks, you can say why plausible scenarios vary so much.\n2. It supports a tail-risk framing. Weitzman and Pindyck both argue that central estimates mislead and that climate policy resembles insurance. That is a reason to stresstest capital against severe scenarios and not only against expected losses. I am drawing that link; none of the four papers mentions banks.\n3. It sets up Layers 5 and 6. Weitzman's stochastic discount factor is the core of asset pricing (Cochrane), and the case for plausible scenarios over false precision is the logic behind the stress-testing work in Layer 6.\n4. It marks a limit of your own study. All four papers use aggregate global damage functions. Translating warming into losses on a particular bank's loan book is not done here, and that gap is the space the thesis works in.\n\nWhat I did not read and file notes\n• Stern: Parts II to VI (about 600 pages). I read the Executive Summary, Chapter 2 and annex, and Chapter 6 pp. 160–161.\n• Weitzman: I read the published 2009 paper in full. The 2007 draft was only compared for differences. His separate review of the Stern Review is not in your folder.\n• Nordhaus: your file is the 2017 DICE-2016R paper, not a 2013 paper.\n• None of the cited papers (Roe and Baker, Dell, Jones and Olken, Torn and Harte) were read.\n\nIf you read one more paper: two cited papers, from their abstracts only\nI read only the abstracts and publisher details for these two, not the papers."},{"pdf":79,"printed":64,"ch":3,"text":"Table 3.12. Master quotables list and how Layer 2 feeds the thesis: If you read one more paper: two cited papers, from their abstracts only Paper\n\nWhat the abstract says\n\nWhy it may matter\n\nDell, Jones and Olken (2012),\n”Temperature Shocks and\nEconomic Growth: Evidence\nfrom the Last Half Century”,\nAmerican Economic Journal:\nMacroeconomics 4(3): 66–95\n(abstract page)\n\nUses year-to-year temperature\nvariation within countries.\nHigher temperatures\nsubstantially slow growth in\npoor countries, and the authors\nargue temperature may affect\ngrowth rates, not only the level\nof output. Effects appear in\nagriculture, industry and\npolitical stability.\n\nThe source for a physical-risk\nchannel to borrower incomes in\npoorer countries. Read the\npaper itself before citing it for\nIndia, since the abstract speaks\nof poor countries in general.\n\nPindyck (2011), ”Fat Tails, Thin\nTails, and Climate Change\nPolicy”, Review of\nEnvironmental Economics and\nPolicy 5(2): 258–274; NBER\nWorking Paper 16353 (NBER\npage)\n\nArgues that fat tails with\nunbounded marginal utility\ncould imply giving up nearly all\nof GDP, and that unbounded\nmarginal utility ”makes little\nsense”. Once marginal utility is\nbounded, expected marginal\nutility stays finite, and a\nthin-tailed distribution can\njustify more abatement than a\nfat-tailed one.\n\nThe direct reply to Weitzman's\nDismal Theorem. Read it if you\nwant to defend a tail-risk\napproach against the objection\nthat the theorem rests on an\nextreme assumption."},{"pdf":80,"printed":65,"ch":3,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 2"},{"pdf":81,"printed":66,"ch":3,"text":""},{"pdf":82,"printed":67,"ch":3,"text":"Notes\n\nLayer 2"},{"pdf":83,"printed":68,"ch":3,"text":""}],"cards":[]};

window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.ln=window.CHTML.ln||{};window.CHTML.ln[3]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">Before you start: what you need to know first</h1>\n<p data-b=\"2\">Layer 2 is one argument told five ways: how much should we spend today to avoid climate damage in the distant, uncertain future? Everything turns on three ideas, so learn these before the papers. The papers themselves do not teach them.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"3\" id=\"b3\">1. Discounting and the Ramsey rule</h2>\n<p data-b=\"4\">A benefit in the future is worth less today, and the \"discount rate\" says how much less. The standard split of that rate is the Ramsey rule:</p>\n<p data-b=\"5\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></math></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"6\"><strong>r</strong> is the consumption discount rate (the number the whole debate is about).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"7\"><strong>ρ</strong> is the pure rate of time preference: how much you value a future person less just for being in the future.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"8\"><strong>η</strong> is the elasticity of marginal utility of consumption: how fast an extra rupee matters less as people get richer. It is also a measure of inequality aversion.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"9\"><strong>g</strong> is the growth rate of consumption per person.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"10\">The richer future people are expected to be, the less a rupee given to them is worth, so a higher g or η raises r. Stern argues for a tiny ρ (about 0.1% a year) and so a low r. Nordhaus argues for a higher ρ and η chosen to match market rates, and so a higher r. Most of their disagreement on how much to cut emissions comes from this one choice, not from climate science.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"11\" id=\"b11\">2. Utility curvature and risk aversion</h2>\n<p data-b=\"12\">In these models utility is usually a power (CRRA) function of consumption, and the same η does two jobs: it sets how much you dislike inequality across time and how much you dislike risk. This is a known weakness, and Weitzman and Pindyck both lean on it.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"13\" id=\"b13\">3. Fat tails</h2>\n<p data-b=\"14\">A \"thin-tailed\" distribution makes extreme outcomes so unlikely that they barely affect averages. A \"fat-tailed\" one leaves enough probability on catastrophe that the extreme outcomes dominate the expected loss. Weitzman’ s argument is that climate sensitivity is fat-tailed because we are uncertain about the uncertainty itself.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"15\" id=\"b15\">Map of Layer 2</h2>\n<table data-b=\"16\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.1.</strong> Before you start: Map of Layer 2</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Section</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">One-line job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stern (2006), the Stern Review</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The case for early, strong action, with a low discount rate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nordhaus (2017), DICE-2016R</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The standard integrated-assessment model; a central social cost of carbon of about US$31 per tonne.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Weitzman (2009), with the 2007 draft</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The Dismal Theorem: fat tails can matter more than the discount rate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pindyck (2013)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Integrated assessment models give false precision; use plausible scenarios instead.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Master list</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The quotes to reach for first and how this layer feeds the thesis.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"17\">How Layer 2 connects to the rest: it answers <em>how much</em> mitigation and <em>when</em>, which is the policy shock that Layer 1’ s scenarios assume and that Layers 3 to 6 turn into bank losses. It does not mention banks.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"18\" id=\"b18\">1. Stern (2006), The Economics of Climate Change: the Stern Review</h1>\n<p data-b=\"19\"><strong>Source:</strong> Stern, N. (2006), <em>Stern Review: The Economics of Climate Change</em>, HM Treasury, 662 PDF pages (your file \"Stern (2006).pdf\"). Page references use the Review’ s own numbering: roman numerals for the Executive Summary (\"ES p. x\"), arabic for the chapters.</p>\n<p data-b=\"20\"><strong>What I read.</strong> I read the Executive Summary in full, Chapter 2 (economics, ethics and climate change) and its technical annex on discounting, and the integrated-assessment section of Chapter 6 (pp. 160–161). I searched the rest for the figures quoted below. I did not read the 600 pages of Parts II to VI (impacts by sector, stabilisation costs by technology, policy design, international action).</p>\n<p data-b=\"21\">The Review is a UK Treasury report, not a journal paper. Its job was to give a policymaker a cost-benefit case for acting early. Almost every later paper in this layer is a reply to it.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"22\" id=\"b22\">The argument in six steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"23\"><strong>Climate change is a market failure on an unprecedented scale.</strong> ES p. i: it is \"the greatest and widest-ranging market failure ever seen\", and the economics must be global, long-horizon, built around risk, and open to \"major, non-marginal change\".</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"24\"><strong>Risks rise sharply with the stock of gases.</strong> The stock was about 430 ppm CO2e against 280 ppm before the Industrial Revolution (ES p. iii). At 550 ppm CO2e there is \"at least a 77% chance\" of exceeding 2°C (ES p. iii). Under business as usual (BAU) the stock could \"more than treble\" by 2100, giving \"at least a 50% risk\" of exceeding 5°C (ES p. iv).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"25\"><strong>The cost of inaction is large.</strong> Taking account of risk, the damage from BAU climate change is \"equivalent to an average reduction in global per-capita consumption of at least 5%, now and forever\" (ES p. x). Adding non-market impacts (health, environment) raises it to 11%; a more responsive climate to 14%; equity weights for poor regions to about 20% (ES p. x). The Review’ s range is 5 to 20% and it says the right figure is \"likely to be in the upper part of this range\" (ES p. x).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"26\"><strong>The cost of action is small.</strong> Stabilising at 500 to 550 ppm CO2e would cost \"around 1% of GDP by 2050\", with a range of -1% to +3.5% from the resource-cost method and -2% to +5% from macro models (ES p. xii–xiv).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"27\"><strong>So act now.</strong> \"The benefits of strong, early action considerably outweigh the costs\" (ES p. ii). The Review adds that there is \"a high price to delay\" (ES p. xv).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"28\"><strong>How much action?</strong> Aim for stabilisation in the range of 450 to 550 ppm CO2e (ES p. xvii). \"Uncertainty is an argument for a more, not less, demanding goal\" (ES p. xvii).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"29\" id=\"b29\">Why the discount rate matters so much here</h2>\n<p data-b=\"30\">The Review argues that the pure rate of time preference should be near zero. It makes this an ethical point in Chapter 2:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"31\">Its simple approach is: \"if a future generation will be present, we suppose that it has the same claim on our ethical attention as the current one\" (p. 30).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"32\">It keeps a small δ only for the chance that a catastrophe (the \"meteorite\" example) ends the world. The value used is 0.1% a year, so the chance of surviving 100 years is 90.5% (Ch. 6, p. 160).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"33\">It sets η = 1, so utility is the log of consumption (Ch. 6, p. 160).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"34\">Ramsey, Pigou and Harrod all called pure time discounting indefensible, in the Review’ s quotations (p. 30–31).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"35\">A low δ does not mean a low discount rate: \"using a low δ does not imply a low discount rate\", and with η = 1.5 and growth of 2.5% the discount rate would be 3.75% even if δ = 0 (p. 47). The Review also notes that the standard single discount rate is only valid for small changes around a given path, not for comparing very different futures (Box 2.2, p. 31).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"36\" id=\"b36\">Other numbers worth having</h2>\n<table data-b=\"37\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.2.</strong> Stern (2006): Other numbers worth having</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Item</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Figure</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Social cost of carbon on BAU path</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">about US$85 per tonne of CO2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. xvi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Social cost of carbon on a 450–550 ppm path</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">US$25–30 per tonne, about a third of BAU</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. xvii</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Net benefit of shifting to the better path</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">order of US$2.5 trillion (present value)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. xvii</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Annual cost of extreme weather by mid-century</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0.5–1% of world GDP, from simple extrapolation</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. viii</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Climate-change cost with 2–3°C warming in older models</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">permanent loss of about 0–3% of world output</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. ix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Climate-change cost with 5–6°C, models with abrupt change</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">average 5–10% loss of world GDP, poor countries over 10%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. ix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Peak and decline for 550 ppm CO2e</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">emissions peak within 10–20 years, fall 1–3% a year</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. xi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Power sector decarbonisation by 2050</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">at least 60%, maybe 75%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. xiii</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"38\" id=\"b38\">What the Review says that touches finance and India</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"39\">Finance (one sentence only): at higher temperatures \"the rising costs of extreme weather events could affect global financial markets through higher and more volatile costs of insurance\" (ES p. viii). The Review does not analyse banks.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"40\">India: glacier melt \"eventually threatening one-sixth of the world’ s population, predominantly in the Indian sub-continent\" (ES p. vi). Warming may cause sudden shifts in regional weather patterns \"such as the monsoon rains in South Asia\" (ES p. vii).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"41\">Fossil fuels: reserves profitable to extract \"are more than enough to take the world to levels of greenhouse-gas concentrations well beyond 750ppm CO2e\" (ES p. xiv). This is the other face of the stranded-asset argument you saw in Layer 1.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"42\" id=\"b42\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"43\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.3.</strong> Stern (2006): Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Climate change is \"the greatest and widest-ranging market failure ever seen\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. i</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Opening the case for a policy-driven transition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The benefits of strong, early action considerably outweigh the costs.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. ii</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Citing the founding cost-benefit case for early action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Averaging across possibilities conceals risks.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. ix</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justifying a stress-test or tail-risk approach instead of expected losses only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Uncertainty is an argument for a more, not less, demanding goal\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. xvii</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Arguing that uncertainty strengthens, not weakens, the case for early action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"there is a high price to delay\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. xv</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Supporting a disorderly or late-transition scenario</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"if you care little about future generations you will care little about climate change\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 47</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Explaining why the discount rate is an ethical choice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the rising costs of extreme weather events could affect global financial markets through higher and more volatile costs of insurance\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. viii</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">An early official statement of the financial channel (one sentence only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the results are specific to the model and its assumptions. They should not be endowed with a precision and certainty that is simply impossible to achieve.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ES p. x</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Quoting the authors’ own caution about the numbers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"44\" id=\"b44\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"45\"><strong>The founding policy argument.</strong> If your thesis says that a policy-driven transition is coming and that banks face transition risk, the Stern Review is the source that made \"act early\" the mainstream economic position. It is the reason you can treat a tightening of climate policy as plausible rather than speculative (my reading).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"46\"><strong>Contested numbers.</strong> The 5–20% and 1% of GDP figures are the most cited and the most criticised in this layer. Use them with the date (2006) and with the criticism from Nordhaus, Weitzman and Pindyck in the following sections.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"47\"><strong>The \"price to delay\" idea.</strong> Late and sharp action costs more than early and gradual action. This is the economic logic behind a disorderly scenario in central bank stress tests (the orderly/disorderly labels are a central-bank convention, not Stern’ s wording).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"48\"><strong>A thin finance link.</strong> One sentence on insurance and financial markets. Do not cite Stern for anything about banks.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"49\"><strong>Can you skip the original?</strong> For the argument, the discount-rate stance and the headline numbers, yes. Open these places yourself: the Executive Summary (ES p. i–xxvii, about 27 pages and readable), Box 2.2 (p. 31) on when discounting is valid, and Chapter 6 pp. 160–161 for the model settings. I did not read Parts II to VI. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"50\" id=\"b50\">2. Nordhaus (2017), Revisiting the social cost of carbon (the DICE model)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"51\"><strong>Source:</strong> Nordhaus, W. D. (2017), \"Revisiting the social cost of carbon\", <em>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</em> 114(7): 1518–1523 (your file \"Nordhaus Dice 2013.pdf\"). <strong>The file name is misleading:</strong> this is the 2017 paper on the revised DICE-2016R model, not a 2013 paper. The 2013 version (DICE-2013R) is only cited here. If you meant the 2013 model, tell me and we can look for that paper. Page references are the PNAS page numbers.</p>\n<p data-b=\"52\"><strong>What I read.</strong> All six pages, including every table. It is short enough that you could read the original in an hour.</p>\n<p data-b=\"53\">Nordhaus is the standard counterweight to Stern. Both use a model of an economy growing over time with climate damage subtracted. They differ mostly in how they treat the discount rate.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"54\" id=\"b54\">Vocabulary</h2>\n<table data-b=\"55\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.4.</strong> Nordhaus (2017): Vocabulary</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning in this paper</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Social cost of carbon (SCC)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the change in the discounted value of economic welfare from an additional unit of CO2-equivalent emissions\" (p. 1518). In plain terms, the damage done by one more tonne of CO2.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IAM</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Integrated assessment model: economy, carbon cycle, climate and damages in one system (p. 1518).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">DICE</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Dynamic Integrated model of Climate and the Economy; a Ramsey growth model with climate investment added (p. 1518).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Welfare discount rate (ρ) vs goods discount rate (r)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">ρ applies to the well-being of different generations and is not observed; r is the return on capital and is observed in markets (p. 1520).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Growth-corrected discount rate</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The goods discount rate minus the growth rate of consumption; the key variable that drives the SCC (p. 1522).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Descriptive vs prescriptive discounting</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Descriptive: choose parameters so that the model reproduces observed savings and returns on capital (Nordhaus). Prescriptive: choose the discount rate on ethical grounds (Stern) (p. 1520).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"56\" id=\"b56\">How the model works, briefly</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"57\">It maximises a social welfare function: the discounted sum of population-weighted utility of consumption, with a power utility function (p. 1518).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"58\">Output is reduced by climate damage and abatement costs, so society trades off consumption now against less damage later (p. 1518–1519).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"59\">Damages are a quadratic function of global mean temperature. The paper says the model \"takes globally averaged temperature change (TAT) as a sufficient statistic for damages\" (p. 1519).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"60\">Damages come out at 2.1% of global income at 3°C and 8.5% at 6°C (p. 1519).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"61\">Equilibrium climate sensitivity is set at 3.1°C for a doubling of CO2 (p. 1520).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"62\">The discount rate averages 4¼% a year to 2100, chosen so the model matches observed returns on investment (p. 1520).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"63\" id=\"b63\">The main results</h2>\n<table data-b=\"64\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.5.</strong> Nordhaus (2017): The main results</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Result</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Figure</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SCC for 2015 emissions, central case</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">US$31.2 per tonne CO2 (2010 US$)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 1518, Table 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Growth of the SCC</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">about 3% a year in real terms to 2050; US$102.5 in 2050</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 1520, Table 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SCC if warming is capped at 2.5°C</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">US$184 (hard cap); US$107 (cap on 100-year average)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 1522, Table 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SCC with Stern-style discounting</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">US$197 for 2015</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 1520, Table 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SCC at a 2.5% discount rate on goods</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">US$128.5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 1520, Table 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">SCC at a 5% discount rate on goods</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">US$19.7</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 1520, Table 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Standard deviation of the 2015 SCC</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">US$32; 10th to 90th percentile range US$7 to US$77</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 1522</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Change from DICE-2013R</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">US$17 to US$31, mainly from the carbon cycle and economic assumptions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">p. 1522, Table 4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"65\">The table of discount rates shows the point of this layer: the same model with a different discount rate gives an SCC anywhere from about US$20 to US$200. The disagreement between Stern and Nordhaus is mostly about this one input.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"66\" id=\"b66\">India</h2>\n<p data-b=\"67\">Table 2 (p. 1521) splits the global SCC by region using output shares. India gets US$2.93 of the US$31.21 (a 9% share in this study; the older RICE, FUND and PAGE models give 12%, 5% and 22%). Nordhaus draws the conclusion that \"there is little agreement on the distribution of the SCC by region\" while each country’ s SCC is \"well below the global total\" (p. 1522). The paper also says that damages in poor regions \"have proven most difficult to develop firm estimates for\" (p. 1523).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"68\" id=\"b68\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"69\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.6.</strong> Nordhaus (2017): Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The most important single economic concept in the economics of climate change is the social cost of carbon (SCC).\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1518</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Introducing the SCC as the link between climate damage and carbon pricing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The SCC is \"the change in the discounted value of economic welfare from an additional unit of CO2-equivalent emissions\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1518</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defining the SCC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"The DICE-2016R model takes globally averaged temperature change (TAT) as a sufficient statistic for damages.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1519</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stating a limitation of aggregate IAMs (no sector or bank-level detail)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The goods discount rate \"should reflect actual economic outcomes\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1520</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Contrasting the descriptive and prescriptive approaches to discounting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"These estimates confirm that there is extremely large structural uncertainty about the SCC even in a single model.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1522</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Supporting a scenario-based rather than a point-estimate approach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the regional estimates are poorly understood, often varying by a factor of 2 across the three models\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1522</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Caution on using regional or India-specific SCC numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Regulations with benefits of more than US$1 trillion \"use the SCC\" (Significance box)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1518</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The SCC is used in real policy, so its level matters</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"70\" id=\"b70\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"71\"><strong>A price path for carbon.</strong> The SCC is the carbon price that an optimising government would eventually set. Transition-risk scenarios need a carbon price path, and this paper gives you a defensible central value (about US$31 in 2015, rising about 3% a year) and a range (US$20 to US$200 depending on discounting). Check how the scenario sets you will use in Layers 3 to 6 choose their carbon prices before relying on this (my suggestion; the paper itself does not discuss scenarios for banks).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"72\"><strong>Why scenarios disagree.</strong> Differences between a gentle and an aggressive policy scenario can come from the discount rate alone, not from the physics.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"73\"><strong>The aggregation limitation.</strong> A single global temperature drives all damages. There is no sector, no region of India and no financial sector. That gap is where your thesis sits.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"74\"><strong>Not about banks.</strong> There is nothing on finance or capital adequacy.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"75\"><strong>Can you skip the original?</strong> For the argument, yes. It is only six pages, so I would read Table 1 (p. 1520), Fig. 3 (p. 1520) and Table 4 (p. 1522) yourself because you may quote them. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"76\" id=\"b76\">3. Weitzman (2009), On modeling and interpreting the economics of catastrophic climate change (the Dismal Theorem)</h1>\n<p data-b=\"77\"><strong>Source:</strong> Weitzman, M. L. (2009), \"On Modeling and Interpreting the Economics of Catastrophic Climate Change\", <em>Review of Economics and Statistics</em> 91(1): 1–19 (your file \"weitzman2009.pdf\"). <strong>Your second Weitzman file, \"Weitzman_2007.pdf\", is the November 2007 working-paper draft of this same paper</strong> (44 pages), not his separate review of the Stern Review. I treat the two together here and cite the published page numbers. The differences are listed below.</p>\n<p data-b=\"78\"><strong>What I read.</strong> The published paper in full (19 pages). I skimmed the 2007 draft for differences and did not read it line by line. The proof of the theorem is only a sketch in the paper, and I have not checked the mathematics.</p>\n<p data-b=\"79\">This is the paper that makes the strongest case that the discount rate fight between Stern and Nordhaus is not the main issue. For a thesis on bank capital, its value is a way of thinking about tail risk and about why expected-loss numbers can mislead.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"80\" id=\"b80\">Vocabulary</h2>\n<table data-b=\"81\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.7.</strong> Weitzman (2009): Vocabulary</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Term</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Cost-benefit analysis (CBA)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Comparing discounted benefits and costs of a policy using expected utility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fat tail</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A distribution whose extreme outcomes die away more slowly than exponentially, e.g. a power law (p. 2, fn 2). Thin tails (normal, gamma) die away faster.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Structural uncertainty</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Uncertainty about the parameters and form of the model itself, not just the random outcome within a known model.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Climate sensitivity (S1)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Eventual warming from a doubling of CO2. IPCC AR4: likely 2°C to 4.5°C, best estimate 3°C, values above 4.5°C \"cannot be excluded\" (p. 3).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">S2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Weitzman’ s \"generalized\" sensitivity that adds heat-triggered feedbacks such as methane release (p. 4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stochastic discount factor (M)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The weight on future consumption in expected-utility pricing: M = β exp(−ηY). E<span>[</span>M<span>]</span> is the price today of a sure unit of future consumption (p. 6–7).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">VSL-like parameter (λ)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A \"value of statistical life\" generalised to catastrophe: how many times per-capita consumption society would give up to avoid extinction-scale loss. It sets a floor on consumption (p. 9–10).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Dismal Theorem</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">If λ can be very large, E<span>[</span>M<span>]</span> goes to +∞ (Theorem 1, p. 10).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"82\" id=\"b82\">The argument</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"83\"><strong>The problem is not discounting alone.</strong> The uniqueness of climate change is the \"deep structural uncertainty in the science coupled with an economic inability to evaluate meaningfully the catastrophic losses from disastrous temperature changes\" (p. 1).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"84\"><strong>Tail probabilities are crude but not small enough to ignore.</strong> Averaging 22 studies behind IPCC AR4, the upper 5% level of climate sensitivity is about 7°C (p. 3). Adding heat-triggered feedbacks (Torn and Harte, whose numbers Weitzman uses), the 5% level rises to about 11.5°C and the 1% level to about 22.6°C (p. 4). He rounds this to a base case: P<span>[</span>ΔT &gt; 10°C<span>]</span> ≈ 5% and P<span>[</span>ΔT &gt; 20°C<span>]</span> ≈ 1% (p. 4).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"85\"><strong>Learning cannot thin the tail.</strong> Because a scale parameter is unknown and we have finite data, the \"posterior-predictive\" distribution has a power-law tail whatever the original distribution was (p. 11). Fat tails come from structural uncertainty alone and do not need to be assumed.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"86\"><strong>The Dismal Theorem.</strong> The probability of a disaster of scale s falls only polynomially in s, but the marginal-utility cost rises exponentially. \"The race to the bottom of the bad tail\" is \"won in the limit every time by the marginal-utility impact\" (p. 11). So expected marginal utility is unbounded unless we close the model with a bound on consumption.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"87\"><strong>Closing the model makes the answer depend on λ.</strong> With η ≈ 2 and λ ≈ 100 the consumption floor is about 1% of current consumption; with λ ≈ 1,000 about 0.1% (p. 10). For λ ≈ 1,000 and a catastrophe probability of 0.005, willingness to pay to avoid it is about 83% of consumption (p. 15).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"88\"><strong>So discounting is secondary.</strong> Theorem 1 \"seems to be saying that no matter what values of β or η are selected\" any big-λ CBA should be presumed affected by fat tails (p. 15). \"The debate about discounting may be secondary to a debate about the open-ended catastrophic reach of climate disasters\" (p. 15).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"89\"><strong>A modest policy conclusion.</strong> Weitzman does not give a number. The theorem points to \"a relatively more cautious approach to GHG emissions\" (p. 13), more effort to learn how fat the tail is, and the idea of climate policy as catastrophe insurance (p. 18). He also warns economists against \"misplaced concreteness\" (p. 18).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"90\" id=\"b90\">The generalised Ramsey formula</h2>\n<p data-b=\"91\">The paper uses the well-known risk-adjusted version of the Ramsey rule from the \"Before you start\" section above:</p>\n<p data-b=\"92\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></p>\n<p data-b=\"93\">Here δ is pure time preference, ημ is the growth term, and the last term is a precautionary-saving effect: more uncertainty about consumption (a larger s) lowers the risk-free rate (p. 7). Weitzman says the effect of δ \"is theoretically overshadowed by the effect of the uncertain scaling parameter s\" (p. 7).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"94\" id=\"b94\">How the 2007 draft differs from the published paper</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"95\">The draft uses only one base-case tail: P<span>[</span>ΔT &gt; 20°C<span>]</span> ≈ 1%. The published paper adds P<span>[</span>ΔT &gt; 10°C<span>]</span> ≈ 5% (draft introduction and section 2; published p. 1 and p. 4).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"96\">The section headings and the Dismal Theorem section match; I did not compare the wording line by line.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"97\">The core theorem and model are the same. Cite the published version in the thesis.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"98\" id=\"b98\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"99\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.8.</strong> Weitzman (2009): Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fat-tailed structural uncertainty \"can readily outweigh the effects of discounting in climate-change policy analysis\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1 (abstract)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Arguing that expected-loss analysis with thin tails understates tail risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"fat-tailed CBA can turn conventional thin-tail-based climate-change policy advice on its head\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justifying a tail-risk or stress-test approach over central estimates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"structural or deep uncertainty is potentially much more of a driving force than discounting or pure risk\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Same, and for distancing your thesis from the discount-rate debate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The tail probabilities are \"wildly uncertain, unbelievably crude ballpark estimates—most definitely not based on hard science\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Being honest about the status of extreme-tail numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the more speculative and fuzzy are the tiny tail probabilities of extreme events, the less ignorable and the more serious is the impact on present discounted expected utility for a risk-averse agent\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The central counter-intuitive result</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"expected stochastic discount factors—which lie at the heart of cost-benefit, asset-pricing, and many other important applications of EU theory—can become arbitrarily large\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">11</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A link to asset-pricing (Layer 5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the artificial crispness conveyed by conventional IAM-based CBAs here is especially and unusually misleading\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">18</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Criticising point estimates from integrated assessment models</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"we may be deluding ourselves and others with misplaced concreteness\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">18</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Same</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"100\" id=\"b100\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"101\"><strong>A basis for tail-risk framing.</strong> If you argue that a bank’ s capital should be tested against severe but plausible climate scenarios and not only against expected losses, Weitzman gives a formal reason: with deep uncertainty, an average can be dominated by the tail. This is my reading of how the result can be used; Weitzman is writing about global welfare, not bank losses.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"102\"><strong>A caution against over-claiming.</strong> The paper is about civilisation-scale catastrophe. A bank’ s loan book is a limited-exposure portfolio, and Weitzman himself says that for \"idiosyncratic uncertainty\" that \"affects only one small part\" of a portfolio, exposure is naturally limited and \"bad-tail fatness is not such a paramount concern\" (p. 11). Do not stretch the Dismal Theorem to say that bank losses are unbounded.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"103\"><strong>A bridge to Layers 5 and 6.</strong> The stochastic discount factor is the core object of asset pricing (Cochrane, Layer 5), and the \"insurance\" framing is the logic behind the central-bank climate stress tests in Layer 6.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"104\"><strong>India.</strong> The only mention is a passing remark that India and China might industrialise faster than projected (p. 4).</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"105\"><strong>Can you skip the original?</strong> For the idea and its implications, yes. The mathematics (Sections III to V, p. 6–12) is hard going and I would not expect you to follow all of it, but you should read the Introduction (p. 1–2), Section VI (p. 12–15) and the Conclusion (p. 18) yourself because they are written in plain language and contain most of the quotes. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"106\" id=\"b106\">4. Pindyck (2013), Climate change policy: what do the models tell us?</h1>\n<p data-b=\"107\"><strong>Source:</strong> Pindyck, R. S. (2013), \"Climate Change Policy: What Do the Models Tell Us?\", NBER Working Paper 19244, July 2013 (your file \"Pindyck (2013).pdf\"; the published version is in the <em>Journal of Economic Literature</em>, September 2013). Page references are the working-paper page numbers, which are 1 to 17 starting at the Introduction.</p>\n<p data-b=\"108\"><strong>What I read.</strong> All of it, including the footnotes. It is 17 pages of text and is written in plain language, so it is the easiest paper in this layer to read directly.</p>\n<p data-b=\"109\">Pindyck’ s answer to the question in the title is the first word of the abstract: \"Very little.\" He is not arguing against climate action. He is arguing that the integrated assessment models (IAMs) that produce numbers like Nordhaus’ s SCC give false precision.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"110\" id=\"b110\">The argument</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"111\"><strong>The inputs are arbitrary and decide the output.</strong> Stern and Nordhaus got very different SCCs because \"the inputs that go into the models are so different\" (p. 4–5). Pindyck reports that Nordhaus (2008) finds an SCC around US$20 or less while Stern finds one above US$200 (p. 4). The modeller \"has a great deal of freedom\", so \"these models can be used to obtain almost any result one desires\" (p. 5).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"112\"><strong>Discount parameters cannot be pinned down.</strong> The standard Ramsey discount rate is R = δ + ηg (p. 7). Stern sets δ ≈ 0 and η = 1, so R is small. Nordhaus (2008) matches market data with δ = 1.5% and η = 2, so R ≈ 5.5% (p. 7). Pindyck calls the rate of time preference \"a policy parameter\" and says economists \"have little to say\" about whether discounting future generations is unethical (p. 6). He notes that the US Interagency Working Group sidestepped the issue with a middle rate of 3% (p. 7–8).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"113\"><strong>Climate sensitivity is uncertain and probably more so than modellers allow.</strong> The IPCC’ s 22 studies give about 7°C at the 95th percentile (p. 9). The strength of the feedbacks behind this is largely unknown and, Pindyck says, may even be unknowable for the foreseeable future (p. 8–9). Modellers who calibrate a single distribution \"have understated our uncertainty\" (p. 10).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"114\"><strong>Damage functions are made up.</strong> IAMs relate temperature to GDP with a loss function such as DICE’ s inverse-quadratic. There is no theory for its shape; the parameters are picked so that losses are small at 2–4°C, where there is \"a rough consensus (perhaps completely wrong)\" (p. 13). Putting 5°C or 7°C into the function \"is a completely meaningless exercise\" (p. 13).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"115\"><strong>The most important driver is the one the models cannot see.</strong> The possibility of a catastrophic outcome is \"an essential driver of the SCC\", and \"IAMs cannot tell us anything about catastrophic outcomes\" (p. 14).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"116\"><strong>What to do instead.</strong> Pindyck proposes a simpler approach, like Cold War nuclear risk analysis: set out a plausible range of catastrophic outcomes (e.g., percentage falls in the capital stock) and plausible probabilities, then compute the present value of averting them (p. 15). Policy is \"a form of insurance\" (p. 16).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"117\"><strong>A practical conclusion.</strong> Not to wait. He points to the argument, citing his own work, that the Interagency Working Group’ s US$21 (updated to US$33) number is a \"rough and politically acceptable starting point\" for a carbon tax, to be revised as we learn more (p. 16–17).</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"118\" id=\"b118\">Where he disagrees with Weitzman</h2>\n<p data-b=\"119\">Pindyck agrees with Weitzman that catastrophe matters most, but not that fat tails alone settle the issue. A footnote says Weitzman (2009) \"has shown that parameter uncertainty can lead to a fat-tailed distribution\" and that this \"suggests that the value of abatement is high\", but Pindyck (2011a) \"shows that a fat-tailed distribution by itself need not imply a high value of abatement\" (p. 10, fn 16).</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"120\" id=\"b120\">A point on growth versus level effects</h2>\n<p data-b=\"121\">Pindyck argues that temperature should affect the growth rate of GDP, not only its level, because some damage is permanent and adaptation spending crowds out investment (p. 12). He cites Dell, Jones and Olken (2012), whose panel of 136 countries over 50 years finds that higher temperatures reduce GDP growth rates but not levels, and Bansal and Ochoa (2011, 2012), who find a negative effect of temperature increases on economic growth (p. 12). I have not read those papers; I only report what Pindyck says they show.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"122\" id=\"b122\">Flag: SCC numbers</h2>\n<p data-b=\"123\">Pindyck quotes an SCC \"above US$200\" for Stern (p. 4). The Stern Review’ s own headline figure was about US$85 per tonne (Section 1 above, ES p. xvi). The US$200 level matches Nordhaus’ s own run with Stern’ s discounting (US$197 in Nordhaus 2017, Table 1). When you cite SCC numbers, say whose model and which assumptions produced them.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"124\" id=\"b124\">Quotables</h2>\n<table data-b=\"125\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.9.</strong> Pindyck (2013): Quotables</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Page</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Where it could be used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"IAM-based analyses of climate policy create a perception of knowledge and precision, but that perception is illusory and misleading.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">abstract</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Criticising point estimates from integrated assessment models</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"these models can be used to obtain almost any result one desires\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Explaining why Stern and Nordhaus disagree</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The rate of time preference is \"a policy parameter\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Treating the discount rate as a choice rather than a fact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the damage functions used in most IAMs are completely made up, with no theoretical or empirical foundation\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">13</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Justifying why you do not rely on aggregate damage functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"IAMs cannot tell us anything about catastrophic outcomes, and thus cannot provide meaningful estimates of the SCC\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">14</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Supporting a scenario or stress-test approach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"it may be that the best we can do is rely on the ‘plausible.’\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">16</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defending plausible-scenario analysis against false precision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"One can think of a GHG abatement policy as a form of insurance\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">16</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Linking climate policy to insurance logic (see also Weitzman)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Higher temperatures \"reduce GDP growth rates but not levels\" (Pindyck’ s summary of Dell, Jones and Olken 2012)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Linking warming to borrowers’ income growth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"126\" id=\"b126\">What it means for your thesis</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"127\"><strong>A defence for using scenarios.</strong> If an examiner asks why you do not use a model-optimal carbon price or damage function, Pindyck gives a published reason: the aggregate models rest on arbitrary choices, so a plausible-range approach is more honest. The scenario sets used by central banks follow the same logic (my reading; Pindyck does not discuss banks).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"128\"><strong>A caution on every number in this layer.</strong> The SCC values of US$10 to US$200, Stern’ s 5–20% and Nordhaus’ s 2.1% at 3°C are all model outputs. Cite them as such.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"129\"><strong>A pointer to the growth channel.</strong> If temperature lowers growth, borrower incomes and repayment capacity suffer over time. That is a physical-risk channel to banks, but you would need the original empirical papers to rely on it.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"130\"><strong>Nothing on finance directly.</strong> The paper does not mention banks or capital.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p data-b=\"131\"><strong>Can you skip the original?</strong> For the argument, yes. Since it is short and clear, I would still read sections 2.2 (p. 5–8) and 3.2 (p. 11–13) yourself. Check every quote against the original before it goes in the thesis.</p>\n<h1 data-b=\"132\" id=\"b132\">5. Master quotables list and how Layer 2 feeds the thesis</h1>\n<p data-b=\"133\">Layer 2 is a four-way argument about how to price climate risk when the future is uncertain. Here is how the four papers line up, followed by the ten quotes I would reach for first.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"134\" id=\"b134\">The four positions side by side</h2>\n<table data-b=\"135\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.10.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 2 feeds the thesis: The four positions side by side</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Stern (2006)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Nordhaus (2017)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Weitzman (2009)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Pindyck (2013)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Main claim</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Benefits of strong, early action outweigh costs</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Central SCC of about US$31 per tonne for 2015, rising about 3% a year</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fat-tailed uncertainty can outweigh discounting</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">IAMs give false precision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Discounting</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">δ = 0.1%, η = 1 (Ch. 6, p. 160)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Chosen to match market returns; about 4¼% a year (p. 1520)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">δ is overshadowed by the effect of uncertainty (p. 7, 15)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Arbitrary; a policy parameter (p. 6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Treatment of tails</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Models risk explicitly; \"averaging across possibilities conceals risks\" (ES p. ix)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reports uncertainty: SD of US$32, 10th–90th percentile US$7–77 (p. 1522)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Central: the Dismal Theorem (p. 10)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Central, but IAMs \"cannot tell us anything\" about it (p. 14)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Policy implication</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Aim for 450–550 ppm CO2e; cost about 1% of GDP (ES p. xii, xvii)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Carbon price at the SCC</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">More caution; policy as catastrophe insurance (p. 13, 18)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Treat policy as insurance; use plausible scenarios (p. 15–16)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"136\"><strong>Where they agree.</strong> Carbon should be priced; uncertainty is large; and nobody here analyses banks or capital.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"137\" id=\"b137\">The ten quotes to reach for first</h2>\n<table data-b=\"138\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.11.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 2 feeds the thesis: The ten quotes to reach for first</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Job in the thesis</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Quote (short, verbatim)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Source and page</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Opening the case for policy</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Climate change is \"the greatest and widest-ranging market failure ever seen\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stern, ES p. i</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Discounting is ethics</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"if you care little about future generations you will care little about climate change\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stern, p. 47</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Averages hide tails</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"Averaging across possibilities conceals risks.\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Stern, ES p. ix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Defining the SCC</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the change in the discounted value of economic welfare from an additional unit of CO2-equivalent emissions\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nordhaus, p. 1518</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Model uncertainty</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"extremely large structural uncertainty about the SCC even in a single model\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Nordhaus, p. 1522</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Tails over discounting</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"structural or deep uncertainty is potentially much more of a driving force than discounting or pure risk\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Weitzman, p. 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Fuzzy tails still matter</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"the more speculative and fuzzy are the tiny tail probabilities of extreme events, the less ignorable and the more serious is the impact\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Weitzman, p. 5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Misplaced concreteness</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"we may be deluding ourselves and others with misplaced concreteness\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Weitzman, p. 18</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Models can say anything</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"these models can be used to obtain almost any result one desires\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pindyck, p. 5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Damage functions</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">\"completely made up, with no theoretical or empirical foundation\"</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pindyck, p. 13</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-b=\"139\">For exact wording and context, see the quotables table in each section above.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"140\" id=\"b140\">How Layer 2 feeds the thesis</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><p data-b=\"141\"><strong>It explains why the scenario numbers disagree.</strong> Differences in carbon prices and damage estimates come mostly from the discount rate and the damage function, not from physics. When you present scenarios from Layer 1 or from central banks, you can say why plausible scenarios vary so much.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"142\"><strong>It supports a tail-risk framing.</strong> Weitzman and Pindyck both argue that central estimates mislead and that climate policy resembles insurance. That is a reason to stress-test capital against severe scenarios and not only against expected losses. I am drawing that link; none of the four papers mentions banks.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"143\"><strong>It sets up Layers 5 and 6.</strong> Weitzman’ s stochastic discount factor is the core of asset pricing (Cochrane), and the case for plausible scenarios over false precision is the logic behind the stress-testing work in Layer 6.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"144\"><strong>It marks a limit of your own study.</strong> All four papers use aggregate global damage functions. Translating warming into losses on a particular bank’ s loan book is not done here, and that gap is the space the thesis works in.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 data-b=\"145\" id=\"b145\">What I did not read and file notes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"146\">Stern: Parts II to VI (about 600 pages). I read the Executive Summary, Chapter 2 and annex, and Chapter 6 pp. 160–161.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"147\">Weitzman: I read the published 2009 paper in full. The 2007 draft was only compared for differences. His separate review of the Stern Review is <strong>not</strong> in your folder.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"148\">Nordhaus: your file is the 2017 DICE-2016R paper, not a 2013 paper.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"149\">None of the cited papers (Roe and Baker, Dell, Jones and Olken, Torn and Harte) were read.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 data-b=\"150\" id=\"b150\">If you read one more paper: two cited papers, from their abstracts only</h2>\n<p data-b=\"151\">I read only the abstracts and publisher details for these two, not the papers.</p>\n<table data-b=\"152\">\n<caption><strong>Table 3.12.</strong> Master quotables list and how Layer 2 feeds the thesis: If you read one more paper: two cited papers, from their abstracts only</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Paper</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">What the abstract says</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Why it may matter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Dell, Jones and Olken (2012), \"Temperature Shocks and Economic Growth: Evidence from the Last Half Century\", <em>American Economic Journal: Macroeconomics</em> 4(3): 66–95 (<a href=\"https://ideas.repec.org/a/aea/aejmac/v4y2012i3p66-95.html\">abstract page</a>)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Uses year-to-year temperature variation within countries. Higher temperatures substantially slow growth in poor countries, and the authors argue temperature may affect growth rates, not only the level of output. Effects appear in agriculture, industry and political stability.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The source for a physical-risk channel to borrower incomes in poorer countries. Read the paper itself before citing it for India, since the abstract speaks of poor countries in general.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Pindyck (2011), \"Fat Tails, Thin Tails, and Climate Change Policy\", <em>Review of Environmental Economics and Policy</em> 5(2): 258–274; NBER Working Paper 16353 (<a href=\"https://www.nber.org/papers/w16353\">NBER page</a>)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Argues that fat tails with unbounded marginal utility could imply giving up nearly all of GDP, and that unbounded marginal utility \"makes little sense\". Once marginal utility is bounded, expected marginal utility stays finite, and a thin-tailed distribution can justify more abatement than a fat-tailed one.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">The direct reply to Weitzman’ s Dismal Theorem. Read it if you want to defend a tail-risk approach against the objection that the theorem rests on an extreme assumption.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","s":[[49,"b1"],[50,"b18"],[53,"b50"],[56,"b76"],[59,"b106"],[62,"b132"]]};